&lt;div class="word-content">
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
.5in'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>A few comments on Romans &lt;b>1-2&lt;/b> relate
to a natural theology. We can distinguish here what may be true from what Paul
is arguing in this passage. Natural theology may be possible, but our
conviction is that Paul does not have that in mind in Romans &lt;b>1&lt;/b>. He means
that natural revelation is sufficient for making people without excuse for &lt;u>leaving&lt;/u>
truth already known; getting to that truth via nature is more difficult. The
first format makes general revelation primary proof while the second makes a
lesser truth claim by saying that it confirms special revelation.   &lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>OVERVIEW OF CREATION AND EVOLUTION</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/evidences/creation-and-evolution/overview-of-creation-and-evolution/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/evidences/creation-and-evolution/overview-of-creation-and-evolution/</guid><description>&lt;style>
